# header-covers.rq (loop B, AXIOM) — LEAF-row header→column covering by label-center-in-column.
# The leaf header row is the row nearest the body: MAX(?atHeaderRow). By the design principle
# (genuine merged headers are PARENTS; the leaf row is 1:1 with body columns), a leaf label belongs
# to exactly the one column that CONTAINS its ink center (tab:inkCenterX, raw geometry from
# headergraph.py — the query stays free of numeric literals): colX0 <= inkCenterX < colX1. This is
# evidence-positive (the label's own center position determines its column), open-world, NO
# symmetrization and NO tuned constant. It is the body-grounded fix for wide single-column labels
# (e.g. "Reference Number"): the label's center sits in one column, so it covers that column only,
# never the neighbours the old ink-extent "Merge & Center" symmetrization over-spanned. Unlike a
# center-in-ink test, it also covers ordinary LEFT-ALIGNED leaf labels whose ink does not reach their
# own column center. A column with no leaf label whose center lands in it stays uncovered here (it may
# be a terminal "short parent" covered by a shallower node); parent (upper) rows keep the
# centering-bounded run extension in headers.py.
# Boundary convention mirrors regions.column_of: colX0 <= center < colX1 (half-open).
# Cross-reference: rowrole._column_containing (src/iladub/etkl/rowrole.py) mirrors this SAME
# half-open containment rule in Python, for placing NON-leaf continuation-row fragments (this
# query itself cannot be reused there — it filters to MAX(?atHeaderRow), the leaf row only). A
# future change to the boundary convention here must be mirrored there too.
# Returns (?hrow ?cellIdx ?cidx): for each leaf header cell (row, cell-index), the covered column index.
PREFIX tab: <https://w3id.org/iladub/tab#>
SELECT ?hrow ?cellIdx ?cidx WHERE {
  { SELECT (MAX(?r) AS ?leaf) WHERE { ?hc a tab:HeaderCell ; tab:atHeaderRow ?r } }
  ?cell a tab:HeaderCell ; tab:atHeaderRow ?hrow ; tab:cellIndex ?cellIdx ; tab:inkCenterX ?center .
  FILTER(?hrow = ?leaf)
  ?gc a tab:GridColumn ; tab:colIndex ?cidx ; tab:colX0 ?cx0 ; tab:colX1 ?cx1 .
  FILTER(?cx0 <= ?center && ?center < ?cx1)
}
